# Global banner

A global banner communicates a site-wide message that is important but does not use the emergency treatment. [Official publishing guidance](https://design-guide.publishing.service.gov.uk/components/global-banner/).

**Reference pattern.** The examples document a local adaptation; they do not add a publishing backend or a new Home Assistant capability.

## Use the shared notice decision

Start with the [message decision guide](../../components/status.html#choose-the-right-message) and the [ordinary banner contract](../../components/notification-banners.html#scope-the-notice-before-choosing-a-variant). This variant adds whole-service relevance and an explicit dismissal lifecycle. It does not supply the permission to create a global native HA notice or make a routine message urgent.

## When to use it

Use a global message for a bounded service interruption or a change that affects the whole interface. State the scope and expected next step. A room-specific device problem belongs with that device; a release announcement usually belongs in update information rather than every dashboard.

## Structure and behaviour

Keep a short heading, one concise paragraph and at most one primary destination. If the message can be dismissed, give the action a visible label and define whether dismissal applies per user, browser or message revision. Important operational faults must remain discoverable after dismissal.

## Home Assistant boundary

The official guide describes a limited display-count cookie for some government campaigns. That is not the civica policy. This reference adds no cookie, cross-site tracking or global HA message injection. Persistence requires an explicit implementation and a documented lifecycle.

